import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/hgb/hgb331.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope – a Sonderdelikt, Not a General Offence" },
        { id: "s3", label: "§331 Nr.1 – Misrepresentation in the Core Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Worked Example – Falsifying a Provision to Improve Reported Results" },
        { id: "s5", label: "§331 Nr.2 – Misrepresentation to the Shareholders' Meeting" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example – a False Asset-Position Presentation" },
        { id: "s7", label: "§331 Nr.3 – Misrepresentation in a Capital Increase Context" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example – Overstating Contributed Assets in a Capital Increase" },
        { id: "s9", label: "§331 Nr.4 – Misrepresentation in Company Formation Documents" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example – a False Founders' Report" },
        { id: "s11", label: "The Mental Element – Why Genuine Negligence Alone Is Not Enough" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Penalty Structure – up to Three Years, or a Fine" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Relationship to IFRS – a Layer IFRS Simply Doesn't Address" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map" },
        { id: "s17", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout danger">A CRIMINAL PROVISION, NOT MERELY A CIVIL ONE: §331 carries genuine imprisonment exposure – up to three years – for deliberately misrepresenting or concealing a company's true condition in exactly the documents this entire poster series has spent so long documenting how to prepare correctly. This is the sharpest possible statement of how seriously HGB treats the integrity of financial reporting.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Everything this series has documented, seen from the criminal-law side</div>
      <div class="principle">Every recognition rule, measurement principle, and disclosure requirement this poster series has covered assumes GOOD FAITH preparation. §331 addresses the opposite scenario: what happens when someone in a position of responsibility deliberately gets it wrong – not through error or genuine disagreement about a judgement call, but through intentional misrepresentation or concealment. This is the sharpest possible enforcement mechanism protecting everything else this series has built.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §331 MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Gläubigerschutz, given criminal teeth</strong> – creditors and shareholders rely on the SAME documents this series has covered; §331 exists specifically to deter deliberate abuse of that reliance</span>
        <span><strong>Klarer Einblick, as a criminal-law floor</strong> – every "true and fair view" provision this series has documented (§243(2), §264(2), §297) has §331 standing behind it as the ultimate, criminal-law-backed guarantee against deliberate distortion</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §331 covers, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>№</span><span>Prohibited conduct</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>1</span><span>Misrepresenting/concealing the company's (or group's) circumstances in the core financial statements</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>2</span><span>Misrepresenting/concealing in presentations to the shareholders' meeting</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>3</span><span>Misrepresenting/concealing material facts in a capital increase context</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>4</span><span>Misrepresenting/concealing the circumstances of company formation</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – a Sonderdelikt, Not a General Offence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Only those in a specific position of responsibility can commit this offence</div>
      <div class="principle">§331 is a Sonderdelikt – a "special offence" that can only be committed by someone holding a SPECIFIC status: members of a Kapitalgesellschaft's management board or executive body, members of its supervisory board, or (for the formation-related offence) the company's founders. An outside party with no such role cannot directly commit this particular offence, though they might separately face liability under general criminal law provisions for fraud or similar conduct.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§331 Nr.1 – Misrepresentation in the Core Financial Statements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Every major document this series has covered, named directly</div>
      <div class="principle">§331 Nr.1 criminalises management board, executive body, or supervisory board members who misrepresent or conceal the company's (or, for a group, the Konzern's) circumstances in the opening balance sheet, the Jahresabschluss, the Lagebericht, the Konzernabschluss, or the Konzernlagebericht – literally naming, as the objects of potential falsification, the SAME documents this entire poster series has spent so many posters documenting how to prepare correctly.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Worked Example – Falsifying a Provision to Improve Reported Results</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Deliberately omitting a known, mandatory provision</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Deliberately omitting a known onerous-contract provision</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Recall the onerous contract example from the earlier §§246–251 poster – a company genuinely knows a supply contract has become loss-making, mandating an €80,000 Rückstellung under §249(1). The company's management board, aware of this, deliberately OMITS the provision from the Jahresabschluss specifically to present a more favourable net income figure ahead of a planned refinancing.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">This is NOT merely a §249 measurement error – the DELIBERATE, knowing omission, done specifically to present a more favourable picture, satisfies §331 Nr.1's misrepresentation element, exposing the responsible board members to imprisonment of up to three years or a fine, ON TOP OF whatever civil consequences (director liability, audit qualification, contract rescission) might separately follow.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§331 Nr.2 – Misrepresentation to the Shareholders' Meeting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Beyond the formal statements themselves – oral or informal presentations too</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§331 Nr.2 extends the SAME prohibition to presentations or overviews of the company's ASSET POSITION given directly to the shareholders' or general meeting – capturing misrepresentation that occurs OUTSIDE the formal, written Jahresabschluss/Lagebericht themselves, such as in a board's own oral or supplementary presentation to shareholders at the annual meeting.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example – a False Asset-Position Presentation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A misleading verbal presentation, independent of the written accounts themselves</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Overstating cash reserves at the annual meeting</div>
        <div class="wk-body">At the annual shareholders' meeting, a management board member verbally presents the company's cash position as "comfortably strong" and specifically overstates available liquid reserves by a material amount, KNOWING this to be false, in order to discourage shareholders from pressing for a planned dividend cut.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Even though this occurs in a VERBAL presentation rather than the written Jahresabschluss itself, the deliberate misrepresentation of the company's asset position directly TO the shareholders' meeting satisfies §331 Nr.2 independently – the offence is not limited to the formal WRITTEN documents alone.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§331 Nr.3 – Misrepresentation in a Capital Increase Context</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Protecting the integrity of a specific, high-stakes corporate action</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§331 Nr.3 addresses AG/KGaA management board members who, in the disclosures or explanations given specifically in connection with a capital increase (Kapitalerhöhung) examination or similar corporate action, misrepresent or conceal material facts – protecting the integrity of a specific, high-stakes moment where new investors are relying on accurate representations to decide whether to contribute fresh capital.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Overstating Contributed Assets in a Capital Increase</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Misrepresenting the value of a non-cash contribution</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Overstating a contributed patent's value</div>
        <div class="wk-body">During a capital increase, a management board member knowingly overstates the value of a patent being contributed as a non-cash (Sacheinlage) contribution in exchange for new shares, in the formal disclosures accompanying the capital increase examination.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The DELIBERATE overstatement of the contributed patent's value in this specific corporate-action context satisfies §331 Nr.3 – new and existing shareholders are entitled to rely on an accurate valuation when the company's own capital structure is being genuinely changed through this process.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>§331 Nr.4 – Misrepresentation in Company Formation Documents</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Extending backward to the company's very founding</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§331 Nr.4 extends this same protection backward to a company's own FORMATION – founders, or members of its executive/supervisory bodies, who misrepresent or conceal the circumstances of the company's formation in the documents that process requires (such as a founders' report, Gründungsbericht) commit the same category of offence.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – a False Founders' Report</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Falsifying the record at the very moment a company comes into existence</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: Concealing a related-party discount in a founders' report</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A company's founders knowingly conceal, in the formation-related Gründungsbericht, that a significant asset contributed to the new company was purchased from a related party at a materially inflated price shortly beforehand – information that would have been genuinely relevant to assessing the fairness of the formation itself.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The deliberate concealment of this material formation circumstance satisfies §331 Nr.4 – the criminal exposure attaches EVEN AT the company's founding, before it has ever prepared an ordinary Jahresabschluss at all.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>The Mental Element – Why Genuine Negligence Alone Is Not Enough</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Intent, not mere error, is what §331 targets</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§331's core verbs – "unrichtig wiedergeben oder verschleiern" (misrepresent or conceal) – carry a genuine INTENT requirement (Vorsatz) under standard German criminal law principles, not mere negligence. A genuinely mistaken measurement judgement, an honest disagreement about a difficult accounting estimate, or an oversight caught and corrected does NOT, by itself, satisfy §331 – the offence targets DELIBERATE falsification or concealment, distinguishing it sharply from the negligence-based Ordnungsgeld/Bußgeld administrative consequences (§334, §335) covered separately elsewhere in this Subsection.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Penalty Structure – up to Three Years, or a Fine</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Genuine imprisonment exposure, not merely a financial consequence</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§331's offences generally carry a penalty of imprisonment (Freiheitsstrafe) of up to THREE YEARS, or a fine (Geldstrafe) – a genuinely severe consequence relative to the purely financial Ordnungsgeld and civil liability mechanisms this series has otherwise documented, reflecting how seriously deliberate financial statement falsification is treated within German criminal law specifically.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Distinguishing a genuine, good-faith accounting judgement call (even one later shown to be wrong) from deliberate misrepresentation carrying the necessary criminal intent</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Proving the DELIBERATE, knowing nature of a specific misstatement in a criminal proceeding, as distinct from establishing that an error simply occurred</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS – a Layer IFRS Simply Doesn't Address</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Criminal enforcement sits entirely outside any accounting standard</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">IFRS itself has NO equivalent provision – it is an accounting standard, not a criminal statute, and simply does not legislate consequences for deliberate falsification. Criminal liability for financial statement fraud, in an IFRS jurisdiction, is governed ENTIRELY by that jurisdiction's own separate general criminal or securities law (comparable in spirit to statutes like the US Sarbanes-Oxley Act's own criminal provisions), never by IFRS itself. §331 is once again part of the "legal infrastructure" layer this series has repeatedly identified sitting BENEATH any accounting standard – genuinely necessary for the whole system to function, but structurally outside what IFRS as a standard-setter could ever address directly.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §331 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>§331 Nr.1 misrepresentation</span><span><span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss</span></span><span>The core documents this whole series has documented</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>§331 Nr.4 misrepresentation</span><span><span class="badge be">Gründungsbericht</span></span><span>Formation-stage documents, before any ordinary Jahresabschluss exists</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §331 connects across HGB Book 3 – and beyond</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§264(2), §297</span><span>The true-and-fair-view standards §331 provides the ultimate criminal-law backstop for</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§331a</span><span>A closely related NEXT provision – false assurance, covering a genuinely different but adjacent form of misrepresentation</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§283 StGB</span><span>The Bankrott (bankruptcy fraud) provision flagged much earlier in this series, addressing a related but distinct insolvency-specific offence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§334–335</span><span>The negligence-based Bußgeld/Ordnungsgeld consequences applying where the same conduct falls short of §331's own intent requirement</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Unrichtig wiedergeben</div><div class="glossary-def">To misrepresent – presenting circumstances inaccurately, one of §331's two core prohibited acts.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Verschleiern</div><div class="glossary-def">To conceal – deliberately obscuring circumstances, §331's other core prohibited act.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Sonderdelikt</div><div class="glossary-def">A special offence that can only be committed by someone holding a specific defined status or role – here, management/supervisory board members or founders.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Vorsatz</div><div class="glossary-def">Intent – the mental element §331 requires; genuine negligence alone does not satisfy the offence.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Gründungsbericht</div><div class="glossary-def">Founders' report – a company-formation document whose falsification triggers §331 Nr.4.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Freiheitsstrafe</div><div class="glossary-def">Imprisonment – the penalty §331 exposes offenders to, up to three years, alongside or instead of a fine.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss</span> Core financial statements
      <span class="badge be">Gründungsbericht</span> Company formation documents
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
